WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:07.690
So right now I live in Boston there on the
East Coast.

00:00:07.690 --> 00:00:11.380
Many of my friends there who have been there
for a while they have a story.

00:00:11.380 --> 00:00:16.440
They all remember the day that this happened.

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:21.600
On August 14, 02003, several states in the
American Northeast and Midwest

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:23.540
And part of Canada

00:00:23.540 --> 00:00:29.660
Experienced what was then the second
biggest black out of all time in the world.

00:00:29.660 --> 00:00:31.750
55 million people lost power.

00:00:31.750 --> 00:00:34.430
Some of them lost power for up to a week.

00:00:34.430 --> 00:00:36.650
And what could cause so much chaos?

00:00:36.650 --> 00:00:40.580
Given that no foreign power has ever taken
down America's electric grid

00:00:40.580 --> 00:00:41.840
Especially on this scale

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:43.940
Who was this master criminal

00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:47.140
Who successfully launched an attack on our own infrastructure?

00:00:47.240 --> 00:00:48.640
The culprit...

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:51.140
Was a tree!

00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:57.120
It was a nice summer day

00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:01.180
So people turned on their air conditioning

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:02.560
Which heated up the wires

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:05.780
And because metal expands when it's heated

00:01:05.780 --> 00:01:07.780
That caused to wires to sag

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:10.160
And it sagged enough that a tree

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:12.160
In Ohio

00:01:12.340 --> 00:01:13.460
Could reach that wire

00:01:13.460 --> 00:01:16.240
And set it all on fire

00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:19.200
Which is okay.

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:22.560
This is actually normal - this happens all
the time.

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:26.729
And this would usually result only in one
station, one power station being knocked out.

00:01:26.729 --> 00:01:32.940
But due to a software bug, they couldn't send
an automated alert to its neighboring power stations

00:01:33.340 --> 00:01:37.440
So it took its electric load and passed it
off to its neighboring power stations

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:40.340
Which caused THEM to overload

00:01:40.340 --> 00:01:42.860
So they passed their load onto their neighbors which caused THEM to overload

00:01:42.860 --> 00:01:46.820
Which created a vicious cycle that
swept throughout the entire Northeast

00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:48.500
Causing...

00:01:48.680 --> 00:01:50.979
A blackout.

00:01:50.979 --> 00:01:55.139
This isn't the only example of something bad
cascading throughout an entire network.

00:01:55.139 --> 00:01:57.329
For example, the 02008 Financial Crisis.

00:01:57.329 --> 00:02:00.369
A handful of banks in the US made some bad
bets.

00:02:00.369 --> 00:02:03.119
And then Greece burns.

00:02:03.119 --> 00:02:07.429
That's because we're living in such a connected
economy - for better and for worse.

00:02:07.429 --> 00:02:10.119
Or think about epidemics.

00:02:10.119 --> 00:02:14.920
One patient zero, a little while later, millions
die.

00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:19.290
That's because we're all really connected—again,
for better or for worse.

00:02:19.290 --> 00:02:23.840
And underlying all these systems is something
called "attractors."

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:29.280
They're called attractors because they attract
the system towards different things.

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:32.470
In this case they all attract the system towards
failure.

00:02:32.470 --> 00:02:37.450
So, imagine you have a ball on an oddly-shaped
hill.

00:02:37.450 --> 00:02:44.170
And let's say that the left-right position
represents how good or bad something is.

00:02:44.170 --> 00:02:50.700
So if the ball's way on the left, that's good,
none of the power stations have failed

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:52.480
And all the banks are still alive.

00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:57.610
But if it falls all the way to the right,
that means that power stations have all failed

00:02:57.610 --> 00:03:00.750
The banks are all bankrupt.

00:03:00.750 --> 00:03:09.260
Also want to clarify that the up-down axis
is not good or bad, it's "stable" or "unstable."

00:03:09.260 --> 00:03:13.480
So if a ball is really high up in the air,
it's pretty unstable.

00:03:13.480 --> 00:03:18.700
But if it's down, grounded close to the earth,
it's pretty stable.

00:03:18.700 --> 00:03:21.000
And now, stable and unstable, again, words
are terrible

00:03:21.160 --> 00:03:23.780
They have positive and negative connotations

00:03:23.860 --> 00:03:27.680
But here's the thing: here we have a good
but unstable situation.

00:03:27.690 --> 00:03:31.330
And also a bad but very stable situation.

00:03:31.330 --> 00:03:35.380
And these mountains are what we saw earlier:
reinforcing loops.

00:03:35.380 --> 00:03:36.750
A ball on a hill.

00:03:36.750 --> 00:03:40.410
So one reinforcing loop in the
power station example is: power station fails

00:03:40.410 --> 00:03:42.520
It cascades on to the next ones.

00:03:42.520 --> 00:03:46.210
Or: a bank fails, and that cascades on to
the next banks.

00:03:46.210 --> 00:03:48.040
It keeps the ball rolling.

00:03:48.040 --> 00:03:52.600
And the valleys, those correspond to balancing
loops.

00:03:52.600 --> 00:03:55.120
Ball in the valley.

00:03:55.120 --> 00:04:00.540
For example, for the power station example,
eventually you run out of power stations to make fail

00:04:00.700 --> 00:04:04.450
And for banks, eventually you run out of banks
to make bankrupt.

00:04:04.450 --> 00:04:08.350
So the ball stops at the bottom of that valley.

00:04:08.350 --> 00:04:10.520
And these valleys are the attractors.

00:04:10.520 --> 00:04:14.380
Because they attract the ball to a certain
point.

00:04:14.380 --> 00:04:17.280
In this case, failure.

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:23.100
So now imagine you are standing next to this
ball, and you're a complete jerk so you push it a little bit

00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:30.820
And with a little small nudge, it gets past
the tipping point, and then gravity takes over

00:04:31.020 --> 00:04:36.000
The ball will clearly roll down the reinforcing
loop of the mountain and into the balancing loop

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:38.460
The valley, the attractor.

00:04:38.460 --> 00:04:40.080
The Valley of Death.

00:04:41.620 --> 00:04:45.860
And furthermore, you can see that if you were
to go down and actually be nice for once

00:04:45.860 --> 00:04:49.960
And try to undo all your damage, it would take
you a lot more effort

00:04:49.960 --> 00:04:55.520
Than what it took you to push the ball in the first place all the way down.

00:04:55.520 --> 00:05:00.560
That's why a blackout can cascade in a few
hours, but fixing it took weeks.

00:05:00.560 --> 00:05:04.480
Or why the recession can spread around the
globe in a few months but fixing it took

00:05:04.540 --> 00:05:07.460
Well, we're still living through the consequences.

